(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["da-easy-2026-013"]={"v":1,"id":"da-easy-2026-013","title":"Mock Level Test-4 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA","sourceTitle":"Mock Level Test-4 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA","branch":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE","code":"DA","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI","seriesId":"da-easy-2026","num":13,"category":"mock","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI"},"source":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_+_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Data_Science_and_AI/025_Mock Level Test-4 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the below question followed by three statements. You have to study the question and the statements and decide which of the statement(s) is/are necessary to answer the given question.<br />\nA, B and C together start a business with a total investment of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/da/da91251fe33dbf1471e7.png\" /> At the end of the year, the total profit is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/22/2211c20705f88f1f7e1e.png\" /> What is A’s share in the profit?<br />\n1. A’s contribution is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/571a28b567c9698614a9.png\" /> times B’s contribution.<br />\n2. B’s contribution is twice that of C.<br />\n3. A’s contribution is thrice that of C.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"All 1, 2 and 3"},{"key":"D","html":"Any two of the three"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/91/91a49c0b45964e671d3e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Find the next term in the alpha-numerical series:<br />\nZ1A, X2D, V6G, T21J, R88M, P445P, ?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"N2676S"},{"key":"B","html":"N2676T"},{"key":"C","html":"T26TON"},{"key":"D","html":"T2676N"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c2/c24006aada2079c59084.png\" /></div>"},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Of the five villages P, Q, R, S and T situated close to each other, P is 1 m to the west of Q, R is 1 m to the south of P, T is 1 m to the north of Q and S is 1 m to the east of T. Then R is in which direction with respect to S?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"North-West"},{"key":"B","html":"South-East"},{"key":"C","html":"South-West"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d8/d80606359c36474cd08a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A conical vessel whose internal radius is 10 cm and height 72 cm is full of water. If this water is poured into a cylindrical vessel with internal radius 30 cm, the height of the water level rises in it is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2f8d9c4280fd58a7bb01.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/81/817475010e991c937c59.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/56/563cf7cb19a824372cbd.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6b/6be0b3a7b62e983f7a1f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>An opaque solid cone is suspendended in the path of a parallel beam of light such that its shadow is cast on a screen oriented perpendicular to the direction of the light beam.<br />\nUnder these conditions, which one of the shadows <em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em> and <em>D</em> is not possible?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3c/3c74dd8bf2da97a6b6f5.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A"},{"key":"B","html":"B"},{"key":"C","html":"C"},{"key":"D","html":"D"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n&bull; A cone&rsquo;s circular base can project a circle when the cone&rsquo;s axis is perpendicular to the screen.<br />\n&bull; Tilt the cone a bit and the circle becomes an ellipse.<br />\n&bull; If the cone is oriented so the apex and base edge project on the screen, the outline can form a<br />\ntriangle C the lateral surface projects as two straight sloping edges meeting at the apex.</p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The sum of all the numbers greater than 10000 formed by using digits 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 (no digit repeated in any number) is equal to?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"5199960"},{"key":"B","html":"5209960"},{"key":"C","html":"5199980"},{"key":"D","html":"5299960"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/16/16b716747e3f8daa98d3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":7,"type":"NAT","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Monica has a piece of canvas whose area is 551 m<sup>2</sup>. She uses it to have a conical tent made, with a base radius of 7 m. Assuming that all the stitching margins and the wastage incurred while cutting, amounts to approximately 1 m2, the volume of the tent is _____ m<sup>3</sup>.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1225,1239],"answerText":"1232","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1232 (1225 - 1239)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5d44770e139a6e3f0d8b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The diameter of a sphere is decreased by 25%. By what percentage does its curved surface area decrease?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"25%"},{"key":"B","html":"37.5%"},{"key":"C","html":"43.75%"},{"key":"D","html":"50%"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nIf the diameter decreases by 25%, the radius also decreases by 25%.<br />\nSo the new radius = 75% of the old radius = 0.75r<br />\nCurved surface area is proportional to the square of the radius : (0.75)<sup>2</sup> = 0.5625<br />\nThis means the new area is 56.25% of the original area<br />\nSo, the decrease = 100 &ndash; 56.25 = 43.75%<br />\nTherefore, the curved surface area decreases by 43.75%.</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A tariff against computers made in country z is needed to protect computer manufactures in this country. With such a tariff, domestic computer manufactures would see increased sales of their own products. And increased sales frequently lead to reduced prices. The conclusion which can<br />\nbe most properly drawn from above statement is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A lack of protective tariff has hampered computer production in this country."},{"key":"B","html":"A tariff would probably lead to a drop in prices for buyers of domestically produced computers."},{"key":"C","html":"Domestic manufacturers will decrease computer production if a protective tariff is not established"},{"key":"D","html":"A drop in computer prices is likely to create more jobs in the country."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":10,"type":"NAT","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>All the soldier are arranged in the form of an equivalent triangle i.e. one soldier in front row, 2 soldier in second row and 3 soldier in third row and so on. If 669 more soldiers from other are added in such a way that all the soldiers form a square and each of the side contains 8 less soldier<br />\nthan each side of equilateral triangle. The number of soldier initially were _________ .</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1540,1540],"answerText":"1540","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1540</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bb/bb4ebe47b4757cc09620.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":11,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> be independent chi-square random variables with degree of freedom <em>p</em>(≥, 3) and<em> q</em>(≥, 3) respectively. If the expected value <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/88/88dc173d17b877b8fd5e.png\" /> is equal to ________. (Upto 2 decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.71,0.71],"answerText":"0.71","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.71</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2b/2b7782be19fc4929487e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":12,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Given two tables <em>R</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>a</em>, <em>b</em>) and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>c</em>, <em>d</em>) with 70 and 50 number of tuples respectively. Find maximum number of tuples in the output of natural join between two tables <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub>.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3500,3500],"answerText":"3500","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3500<br />\n&bull; Natural join tests the equality condition between the relation using common attribute. If no<br />\ncommon attribute it gives cross product.<br />\n<em>R</em><sub>1</sub> has 70 tuple and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> has 50 tuples.<br />\nSo, max tuples will be 70 &times; 50 = 3500</p>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>What is the time complexity of following function fun( )? Assume that log(x) returns log value in base 2.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b1/b169e1f1f724a1a577bd.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>T</em>(<em>n</em>)"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>T</em>(<em>n </em>log log <em>n</em>)"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>T(n</em> log <em>n</em>)"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>T</em>(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>)"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/59b515ccdbe5c127d63d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>If the system of equations 2<em>x</em> + 3y – z = 0, <em>x</em> + ky – 2z = 0 and 2<em>x</em> – y + z = 0 has a non-trivial solution (<em>x</em>, y, z), then <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/f0/f0b1d6697056d0b0e8e8.png\" /> is equal to</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"–4"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5b/5b38574307b55263fa7c.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c8/c8408d95966f0088c63a.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/47/47503a3c20b6575a3c57.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ff/ff599cd902e171f058a3.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a2/a211e47e2216f315e288.png\" /></div>"},{"n":15,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Find the value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/2e/2ea49b9a9b161868f72e.png\" /> ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.5,0.5],"answerText":"0.5","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.5</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5a/5acaaa1c17ddb9b6de8c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A dataset with 80 samples is evaluated using k fold cross-validation, where the training set size in each iteration is exactly three times the validation set size.<br />\nWhat is the value of <em>k</em>?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"4"},{"key":"B","html":"3"},{"key":"C","html":"5"},{"key":"D","html":"6"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dd/ddfba40aa6c3a2927791.png\" /></div>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : The ReLU activation function is bounded above and has a finite maximum value.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : The F1-score of a binary classifier attains its maximum value only when both precision and recall are equal to 1.<br />\nWhich of the following is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; ReLU is defined as max(0, x) and is unbounded above; hence it has no finite maximum. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>&nbsp;is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; Since F1-score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, it equals 1 if and only if both precision and recall are equal to 1. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is correct.</p>"},{"n":18,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Depth-limited search algorithm uses the stack data structure."},{"key":"B","html":"Depth-first search (DFS) always finds a solution in infinite-depth search spaces without depth limit."},{"key":"C","html":"Uniform-cost search (UCS) may fail to find the least-cost solution if a stack is used instead of a priority queue."},{"key":"D","html":"UCS always finds a solution even if step costs are negative."}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)<br />\n&bull; Depth-limited search is a DFS variant, and DFS uses a stack (explicitly or via recursion) to<br />\nkeep track of nodes. So, option (a) is correct.<br />\n&bull; Plain DFS can get stuck in infinite branches; without a depth limit, it is not guaranteed to find<br />\na solution. So, option (b) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; UCS requires a priority queue to always expand the lowest-cost node first. Using a stack<br />\nwould break this property. So, option (c) is correct.<br />\n&bull; UCS assumes non-negative step costs. Negative costs can cause cycles and UCS may fail to<br />\nterminate or find the correct solution. So, option (d) is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":19,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>A balanced search tree of order 6 allows each internal node to have at most 6 children, and every non-root node must have at least <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/85/85ce4916e798b7bc013d.png\" /> children. The height of the tree is defined as follows: a tree with one level has height 0, a tree with two levels has height 1, and so on. If the tree has a height of 4, what is the minimum number of distinct keys that can be stored in it?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[161,161],"answerText":"161","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>161</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/86/86b2caf80bb61527ba80.png\" /></div>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider an initially empty double-ended queue. The following operations are executed:<br />\ninsertLast(18)<br />\ninsertFirst(7)<br />\ninsertLast(30)<br />\nremoveFirst( )<br />\ninsertFirst(12)<br />\nremoveLast( )<br />\nWhich of the following represents the correct order of elements removed?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"7, 18"},{"key":"B","html":"18, 30"},{"key":"C","html":"7, 30"},{"key":"D","html":"12, 30"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":21,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d0/d000a5d05c7eec9dfef0.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is true?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a3/a37c9d8a9b9509d6ae27.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/eb/eb55c6de3ff1ab00ef62.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f9/f98a471f1c15ce96168e.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fe/fec27e78e4436039f519.png\" />"}],"answer":["B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/66/66f88f8dca7011b65b62.png\" /></div>"},{"n":22,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>If M is a non-singular matrix such that MM<sup>T </sup>= M<sup>T</sup>M and N = M<sup>–1</sup> M<sup>T</sup>, then matrix N is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Involuntary"},{"key":"B","html":"Idempotent"},{"key":"C","html":"Orthogonal"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/55/559ab11910b9c03fc621.png\" /></div>"},{"n":23,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only preorder traversal of a binary tree is given, then we can construct unique binary tree."},{"key":"B","html":"Inorder traversal of Binary search tree is always sorted order."},{"key":"C","html":"Deleting a node with two children in Binary search tree can be done by replacing the node with either its inorder predecessor or inorder successor."},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c)</p>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>1</sub> :</strong> A* searching algorithm takes less space than best first search.<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>2</sub> :</strong> A* searching always gives the optimal solutionst.<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>3</sub> :</strong> Best First Search does use information from previous steps.<br />\nWhich of the following is incorrect?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Only S<sub>1</sub>, S<sub>2</sub> and S<sub>3</sub>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Let G = (V, E) be a directed weighted graph with n vertices. The Bellman-Ford algorithm is executed from source s. For a vertex <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/fd/fd8dd0edf3e963a1f857.png\" /> denote the distance estimate after i complete iterations of edge relaxations.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : If no edge can be relaxed in the <em>n</em><sup>th</sup> iteration (i.e. after performing the usual <em>n</em> – 1 iterations we check the <em>n</em><sup>th</sup> and find no relaxation possible), then G has no negative-weight cycle reachable from s.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : If there exists a vertex <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/dc/dcd41b6e869c781a8675.png\" /> must lie on a negative-weight cycle.<br />\nWhich one is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is true"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is true"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are true"},{"key":"D","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are false"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":26,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>What will be the output of given below Python code:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f2/f2f2c0aabc83d2415070.png\" /></div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[5,5],"answerText":"5","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>5</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ba/bafa1acf205c3aa24935.png\" /></div>"},{"n":27,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A fair die is rolled repeatedly until two successive sixes occur. What is the probability that the process terminates on the fifth roll?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.022,0.022],"answerText":"0.022","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.022</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/49/49d340474c7d26a4d3fe.png\" /></div>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<strong>I.</strong> If a relation R is in 3NF but not BCNF, then R has a compound key.<br />\n<strong>II.</strong> A relation consisting of only prime attributes is in 3NF.<br />\nWhich of the above is true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"I only"},{"key":"B","html":"II only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both I and II"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nBoth I and II are standard results.</p>"},{"n":29,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider a circular queue implemented using an array <em>A</em>[0 ........ <em>n</em> – 1] of size n. Which of the following is incorrect for this circular queue?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Enqueue operation insert an element at rear end of queue."},{"key":"B","html":"Dequeue operation takes Ο(<em>m</em>) time, where m is number of elements in queue."},{"key":"C","html":"Overflow condition of queue is (Rear + 1) % <em>n</em> == front."},{"key":"D","html":"All of the above"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nDequeue operation takes place at front end, and it takes &Omicron;(1) time.</p>"},{"n":30,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the function <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/25/25f26d789575c6d582e2.png\" /><br />\nIdentify which of the following statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cc/cc71725294a9b8773efe.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/26/2675e41059e1a95f2de8.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ef/ef638db7f249b5aa0013.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/92/92de96405486b2a78fdd.png\" />"}],"answer":["A","B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a9/a9ed6ff75ab5484f8d72.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a0/a0a306147a5b97e111f6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":31,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A search algorithm is defined as follows:<br />\n• First, it performs a Linear Search on the first <em>k</em> elements of a sorted array of size <em>n</em>.<br />\n• If the key is not found in the first <em>k</em> elements, it performs a Binary Search on the remaining (<em>n</em> – <em>k</em>) elements.<br />\nAssume that the key is present uniformly at random in the array.<br />\nIf all operations (comparison, jump, etc.) take unit time, what is the expected number of comparisons<br />\nrequired to search for the key using this algorithm?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4b/4b5e61d42ed8b5286037.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/af/af4f85b5ccd8037d9067.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9d/9de7375e56d89a81b44a.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"log<sub>2</sub>(<em>n</em>)"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ff/ff020ab6e256898028af.png\" /></div>"},{"n":32,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the one-dimensional data set:<br />\n{18, 4, 23, 7, 11, 1, 10, 9, 15, 3}<br />\nThe initial centroids for the k means clustering algorithm are chosen as:<br />\n<em>K</em><sub>1</sub> = 4 and <em>K</em><sub>2</sub> = 20<br />\nAfter one iterations of the k means clustering algorithm, determine the values of the centroids.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(7, 20)"},{"key":"B","html":"(6, 19)"},{"key":"C","html":"(8, 20)"},{"key":"D","html":"(6, 20)"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/69/69c68491d409ac271ec7.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7b/7b61a0f4fc90ef4e2352.png\" /></div>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>In hierarchical agglomerative clustering, the distance between two clusters <em>C</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>C</em><sub>2</sub> is defined as</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/8200733da54e69f71b47.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>where <em>d</em>(⋅ , ⋅) denotes the Euclidean distance between data points.<br />\nWhich of the following linkage criteria uses the above definition?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Single linkage"},{"key":"B","html":"Complete linkage"},{"key":"C","html":"Average linkage"},{"key":"D","html":"Ward’s method"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":34,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>The rank of the matrix <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/a9/a9e9065e31473f58788e.png\" /> is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f9/f9cd764591f0cc2dd50d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":35,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a search tree with branching factor <em>b</em> and solution at depth <em>d</em>. Which statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"BFS has space complexity Ο(<em>b<sup>d</sup></em>)."},{"key":"B","html":"DFS has space complexity Ο(<em>b</em> ⋅ <em>d</em>)."},{"key":"C","html":"Depth-limited search has space complexity Ο(<em>b<sup>d</sup></em>)."},{"key":"D","html":"UCS has space complexity proportional to the number of nodes in the frontier, which may be exponential."}],"answer":["A","B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, d)<br />\n&bull; BFS stores all nodes at the current level; space grows exponentially with depth d. So, option<br />\n(a) is correct.<br />\n&bull; DFS only stores nodes along the current path; space is &Omicron;(<em>b</em> &sdot; <em>d</em>). So, option (b) is correct.<br />\n&bull; Depth-limited search stores nodes along the path, so space is &Omicron;(b &sdot; <em>l</em>), not &Omicron;(<em>b<sup>d</sup></em>). So, option (c) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; UCS stores all frontier nodes in a priority queue; space can be exponential in worst case.<br />\nSo, option (d) is correct.</p>"},{"n":36,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub>: It is preferable to use dense index rather than sparse index when the file is not sorted on the indexed field.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : If index entries are sorted in ascending order, the occupancy of each leaf node of a B<sup>+</sup> tree (except the last leaf node) is about 50%.<br />\nWhich of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only S<sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Only S<sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; It is preferable to use a dense index instead of sparse when the file is not sorted on index field<br />\n(such as when the index is a secondary index) or when the index file is small compared to the size of memory.<br />\n&bull; If index entries are inserted in ascending order, the new entries get directed to the last leaf<br />\nnode. When this leaf node gets filled, it is split into two. Of the two nodes generated by split,<br />\nthe left node is left untouched and the insertions take place on the right node. This makes the<br />\noccupancy of the leaf nodes to about 50% except the last leaf.</p>"},{"n":37,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> be two random variables having the joint probability density function:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ca/caef991e9f2767727483.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Find the conditional probability <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/aba7983eb2bf3803daa8.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ae/ae7c9af3826d96925224.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fb/fb26984d8a4f876ef47b.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/50/50bc2465ebe3d759010a.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1a/1a49d3a5f1309799bfdd.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/46/460e16d94c3d3013e373.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5b/5bc317c93d230edf7eef.png\" /></div>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider a two-class classification problem where the class-conditional distributions are Gaussian with equal covariance matrices.<br />\nWhich classifier is theoretically equivalent to LDA under this assumption?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Naïve Bayes"},{"key":"B","html":"Logistic Regression"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>k</em>-Nearest Neighbors"},{"key":"D","html":"Support Vector Machine with RBF Kernel"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a4/a4a1432c2248c9d3ffc9.png\" /></div>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a relational database schema with two tables:<br />\n1. DEPARTMENTS<br />\n• DeptID (Primary Key, NOT NULL)<br />\n• DeptName<br />\n2. EMPLOYEES<br />\n• EmpID (Primary Key, NOT NULL)<br />\n• EmpName<br />\n• Mgr ID (Foreign Key referencing EmpID in EMPLOYEES)<br />\n• Dept ID (Foreign Key referencing DeptID in DEPARTMENTS)<br />\nNow, assume the following two specific constraints are applied to the foreign key on<br />\nEMPLOYEES.DeptID:<br />\n1. EMPLOYEES.DeptID is constrained as NOT NULL.<br />\n2. The foreign key definition includes the Referential Action: ON DELETE SET NULL.<br />\nA new department is created with DeptID = 100. An employee is hired and assigned to this<br />\ndepartment: EmpID = 500, DeptID = 100.<br />\nWhich of the following database operations will result in a Referential Integrity Error?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"INSERT INTO EMPLOYEES (EmpID, DeptID) VALUES (501, 101); (Where DeptID = 101 does not exist)"},{"key":"B","html":"UPDATE EMPLOYEES SET DeptID = NULL WHERE EmpID = 500;"},{"key":"C","html":"DELETE FROM DEPARTMENTS WHERE DeptID = 100;"},{"key":"D","html":"UPDATE DEPARTMENTS SET DeptID = 102 WHERE DeptID = 100; (Assuming no ON UPDATE action is specified)"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n1. The DELETE operation targets the department that is currently referenced by EmpID = 500.<br />\n2. The Foreign Key&rsquo;s Referential Action is triggered: ON DELETE SET NULL.<br />\n3. The database attempts to automatically change the EMPLOYEES. DeptID value from 100 to NULL.<br />\n4. This attempt immediately violates the NOT NULL constraint explicitly placed on the<br />\nEMPLOYEES.DeptID column.<br />\n5. Because the database fails to execute the referential action, the original DELETE statement is<br />\nrolled back and an Referential Integrity Error is raised.<br />\nThis operation tests the most advanced interaction: that a NOT NULL constraint on the referencing<br />\ncolumn overrides an ON DELETE SET NULL action.</p>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let <em>V</em> be the column space of the matrix <em>A</em> = <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/85/85afa90596339599df40.png\" /> Then the orthogonal projection of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/2a/2ae6d30bc0a431f806ae.png\" /> on <em>V</em> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bc/bcc029f5ca5ad4edad2e.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/51/51b0d69a0178eadb888b.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7ed4905a945a8b69272e.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3f/3f66684634ca25f1cba7.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d9/d97d99d1fa55b5219ed8.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6f6259d2b5b99393c942.png\" /></div>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which one of the following is the most appropriate logical formula to represent the statement:<br />\n“The conjunction of two tautologies is a tautology”.<br />\nThe following notations are used:<br />\n<strong><em>T</em>(<em>x</em>):</strong> <em>x</em> is a tautology.<br />\n<strong><em>C</em>(<em>x</em>):</strong> <em>x</em> is a contradiction.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2f589ecac133add151d5.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/08/0866c8fddc317610016b.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/35/35c552382844c5b24bda.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/eb/eb5eb5b369f6f0793bfe.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9d/9d650c823e1d3e58608b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider 2 × 2 matrix <em>A</em> given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d0/d0bf22de16dee7fc89ec.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Find the eigen values of matrix <em>A</em>.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(2, 6)"},{"key":"B","html":"(1, 3)"},{"key":"C","html":"(3, 7)"},{"key":"D","html":"(2, 7)"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/db/dbe4755ea6239d1733f0.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2a/2ad4baf9f5dc250a39f2.png\" /></div>"},{"n":43,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>For a dataset of annual rent <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/7c/7c297277dcc4ba4e435d.png\" /> of apartments, the minimum, maximum, mean, and standard deviation are <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/a3/a36bbc1f72ce8beab0d5.png\" /> respectively.<br />\nThe Z-score normalized rent value of `45,000 is closest to which option?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.5"},{"key":"B","html":"1.0"},{"key":"C","html":"1.2"},{"key":"D","html":"1.5"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/48/482968101b61bc30cd38.png\" /></div>"},{"n":44,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following option is correct for the given predicate with domain of all people</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b3/b3350c015caa2381bbdf.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Where <em>F</em>(<em>x</em>, y) means ‘<em>x</em> is a friend of y’?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"If two persons are not friend of each other then they have atleast one friend common."},{"key":"B","html":"If two persons have atleast one common friend then they are not friend of each other."},{"key":"C","html":"Both (a) and (b)"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7a/7aad99bab230603c99b1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":45,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statement(s) is/are true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Quicksort and merge sort are both examples of divide and conquer algorithms."},{"key":"B","html":"If we randomly choose a pivot element each time, quicksort will always terminate in time Ο(<em>n</em> log <em>n</em>)."},{"key":"C","html":"For every fixed strategy to choose a pivot for quicksort, we can construct a worst case input that requires time Ο(<em>n</em>)."},{"key":"D","html":"If we could find the median in time Ο(<em>n</em>) and median is chosen as pivot, quicksort would have worst case complexity Ο(<em>n</em> log <em>n</em>)."}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)<br />\nEven with a random choice of pivot in each iteration, we can only guarantee that the expected<br />\ntime is &Omicron;(<em>n</em> log <em>n</em>), not the worst-case. All other statements are correct.</p>"},{"n":46,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Suppose <em>X</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>X</em><sub>2</sub>, ....., <em>X</em><sub>16</sub> represent the operating times (in hours) of a system. Each observation follows a normal distribution with mean (μ) = 12 and Standard deviation (σ) = 8.<br />\nLet <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/66/66d064bfa8b72a1bd00b.png\" /> be the sample mean of the 16 observations then X is also normally distributed. Assume that Z denotes a standard random variable with mean 0 and Standard deviation 1.<br />\nConsider the hypotheses:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d3/d3d316b8ffc4fb0f189d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following expression correctly represents the Type-1 error probability?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e3/e3a24358c1f1d0f04d4a.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/18/18f5a1706badf69cb80f.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/36/36361571cb750da504b8.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2f4ea63ba1cd8042d7f8.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/00/00f45c0f9a14fbcdbc9a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":47,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : After splitting a decision tree node, the entropy value decreases<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : The feature chosen for splitting is the one that maximizes the decrease in entropy.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and S<sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; Splitting divides a node into subsets that are more &ldquo;pure,&rdquo; reducing disorder. This decrease<br />\nin entropy is the basis for information gain used in decision trees. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n&bull; Decision tree algorithms select the feature with highest information gain. This ensures the<br />\nsplit reduces uncertainty the most, leading to a better tree. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is correct.</p>"},{"n":48,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following is logically implied by ∃x (p(x) ∧ s(x))?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ae/ae123eb4c8143b990911.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/89/89031f97671f78dac952.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/31/314c02cf8cfc9be778a6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":49,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding discretization of continuous attributes in data mining:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Supervised discretization uses class labels to determine interval boundaries, improving predictive performance.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Unsupervised equal-frequency discretization guarantees that all intervals have exactly the same width.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; Supervised discretization uses the class labels to decide where to split the attribute values,<br />\noften improving classification accuracy. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n&bull; Equal-frequency discretization divides the data so that each interval has approximately the<br />\nsame number of data points, not equal width. Equal-width discretization divides the range<br />\ninto intervals of equal width. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>&nbsp;is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":50,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A linear-probing hash table of length 10 uses the hash function <em>h</em>(<em>x</em>) = <em>x</em> mod 10. After inserting six integer keys into an initially empty hash table, the array of keys is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/18/18d81a33c53ee6fa5574.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following choice is insertion sequence resulting in the above hash table? Assume that the length of the hash table does not change during the insertions.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"46, 42, 34, 52, 23, 33"},{"key":"B","html":"34, 42, 23, 52, 33, 46"},{"key":"C","html":"46, 34, 42, 23, 52, 33"},{"key":"D","html":"42, 46, 33, 23, 34, 52"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/54/54390fa25b24d798c326.png\" /></div>"},{"n":51,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let {<em>X<sub>n</sub></em>}<sub><em>n</em> ≥ 1</sub> be a sequence of independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) random variables<br />\nwith <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/60/60452815cd923670256f.png\" /></p>\n\n<p>If <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/7d/7d4a59e957e2d079b298.png\" /> find <em>c</em><sup>2</sup> ________. (Upto 1 decimal place).</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1.5,1.5],"answerText":"1.5","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1.5</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4e/4e314188ebf79fe0d727.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cf/cfd2dada91b33f6470ba.png\" /></div>"},{"n":52,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a Bayesian network converted into a junction tree. Which of the following statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Each node of the junction tree corresponds to a maximal clique of the moralized graph."},{"key":"B","html":"Junction tree sampling guarantees exact inference for any query."},{"key":"C","html":"Messages in a junction tree are passed according to the sum-product algorithm."},{"key":"D","html":"Junction tree sampling is always faster than Gibbs sampling."}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)<br />\n&bull; Each junction tree node corresponds to a maximal clique in the moralized and triangulated<br />\ngraph, which ensures consistent belief propagation across the network. So, option (a) is correct.<br />\n&bull; Sampling on a junction tree is approximate; only full message passing guarantees exact inference. So, option (b) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; Messages are passed between cliques using the sum-product algorithm, allowing computation of marginals efficiently. So, option (c) is correct.<br />\n&bull; Junction tree sampling is not always faster than Gibbs sampling; efficiency depends on clique<br />\nsizes and network structure. So, option (d) is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":53,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>In an analysis of court cases, it is found that 50% of the individuals were sent to prison.<br />\nAmong those sent to prison, 30% chose to plead guilty. Among those not sent to prison, 60% chose to plead guilty.<br />\nIf an individual is randomly selected and it is then found that the individual entered a guilty plead, find the probability that the individual was not sent to prison ________. (Up to 3 decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.667,0.667],"answerText":"0.667","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.667</p>\n\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/70/7081efab59ddf70403ee.png\" /></p>"},{"n":54,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Let <em>A</em> be 3 × 3 singular matrix and suppose that 2 and 3 are eigen values of <em>A</em>. Then the number of linearly independent of <em>A</em><sup>3</sup> + 2<em>A</em> + λ<sub>3</sub> is equal to</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1"},{"key":"B","html":"2"},{"key":"C","html":"3"},{"key":"D","html":"4"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/93/932895206e8d9a1fc212.png\" /></div>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is correct about ROLAP (Relational OLAP)?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"ROLAP stores data in multidimensional cubes only."},{"key":"B","html":"ROLAP queries are slower because they compute aggregates on the fly from relational tables."},{"key":"C","html":"ROLAP does not support standard SQL queries."},{"key":"D","html":"ROLAP always gives faster query response than MOLAP."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nROLAP uses relational databases to store data. Aggregates are computed at query time, making<br />\nqueries slower than MOLAP.<br />\nIt supports SQL queries, and it does not store data in multidimensional cubes</p>"},{"n":56,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The following sequences represent node visits in a Binary Search Tree (BST):<br />\n<strong>I.</strong> 15, 29, 37, 45, 52, 66, 74, 81, 93<br />\n<strong>II.</strong> 52, 29, 15, 37, 45, 74, 66, 81, 93<br />\n<strong>III.</strong> 15, 37, 45, 29, 66, 74, 81, 93, 52<br />\n<strong>IV.</strong> 52, 29, 15, 37, 45, 74, 66, 93, 81<br />\nWhich of the following statements correctly describes the traversal type?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Sequence I can be the in-order traversal of a BST having 52 as root."},{"key":"B","html":"Sequence II can represent a pre-order traversal of a BST with 52 as root."},{"key":"C","html":"Sequence III can correspond to a post-order traversal of a BST with 52 as root."},{"key":"D","html":"Sequence IV cannot represent any valid traversal order of a BST."}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)</p>"},{"n":57,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><em>A</em> can hit a target three times in 5 shots, <em>B</em> two times in 5 shots and <em>C</em> three times in 4 shots. Each of them fire one shot simultaneously to the target. Then the probability that at least two shots will hit the target is:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e3/e322fdc034fd01fe6778.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/37/370b48a4a1b63f61a849.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/de/deb34ce8e899d4cbf849.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0c/0c9644afe034d53695cb.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/50/503a68765fe9d1f097e1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Suppose <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/92/92a89de13f20bf774456.png\" /> element is selected as the pivot element by some algorithm in θ(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>) time. If this algorithm is used to select the pivot element then find the worst case time complexity of quick sort.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"θ(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>)"},{"key":"B","html":"θ(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup> log <em>n</em>)"},{"key":"C","html":"θ(<em>n</em><sup>3</sup>)"},{"key":"D","html":"θ(<em>n</em> log <em>n</em>)"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/95/9506df1c91cfb723db83.png\" /></div>"},{"n":59,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let M and N be two 3 × 3 matrices such that MN = NM. Further if M ≠ N<sup>2</sup> and M<sup>2</sup> = N<sup>4</sup>.<br />\nConsider the following statement:<br />\n<strong>I.</strong> Determinant of (M<sup>2</sup> + MN<sup>2</sup>) is 0.<br />\n<strong>II.</strong> Determinant of (M<sup>2</sup> + MN<sup>2</sup>) ≥ 1.<br />\n<strong>III.</strong> There is a 3 × 3 non-zero matrix U such that (M<sup>2</sup> + MN<sup>2</sup>) U is the zero matrix.<br />\nHow many statements are correct?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2,2],"answerText":"2","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d3/d367af9276ee4f07f04a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":60,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The number of binary search trees possible with n nodes such that each nodes takes a value from the set {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6} root has value 3 and the left child of the root has value 2, is equal to ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[5,5],"answerText":"5","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>5</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/35/351ad513bbf2847d275b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":61,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>R</em> be the relational schema with <em>n</em> attributes, <em>R</em>(<em>A</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>A</em><sub>2</sub>, ..... <em>A</em><sub>n</sub>) consider the following statements and identify the true statements?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Maximum number of candidate key possible over relational schema is ‘<em>n</em>’."},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/d7/d759c44d1dfa31cc7457.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"Maximum number of super key occurs when only <em>A</em><sub>1</sub> is candidate key."},{"key":"D","html":"Maximum number of super key occurs when every single attribute is candidate key."}],"answer":["B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/84/84d1b492f4579752621b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":62,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The matrix <em>P</em> = <em>WW<sup>T</sup></em> acts as a projection operator. Which of the following is a key property of this projection matrix?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>P</em><sup>2</sup> = <em>P</em>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>P<sup>T</sup></em> = –<em>P</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>P</em><sup>–1</sup> = <em>P</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"det(<em>P</em>) = 1"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nApplying the projection twice (<em>WW<sup>T</sup> WW<sup>T</sup></em>) is the same as applying it once because <em>W<sup>T</sup> W</em> = <em>I</em>.</p>"},{"n":63,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The value of the integral given below is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/13/1329c4e9079eec340d56.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/73/73b694abc5715a13feb0.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/19/19569ad69791676886fe.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/07/072c4f203693bdc0dcc4.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e2/e2ed3848f1e336774ddc.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9b/9ba89b838f9433001a90.png\" /></div>"},{"n":64,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In Linear Discriminant Analysis, the optimal projection matrix W is obtained by maximizes</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e8/e843eb99470f33f0a6c9.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c2/c20a86f77c9451543497.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following statements is TRUE?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>S<sub>w</sub></em> captures the dispersion of class means."},{"key":"B","html":"<em>S<sub>B</sub></em> measures dispersion of samples within each class."},{"key":"C","html":"LDA seeks directions that maximize class separability."},{"key":"D","html":"LDA minimizes total variance of the projected data."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; The objective function of LDA maximizes the ratio of between-class variance to within-class variance.<br />\n&bull; This ensures that:<br />\n(i) Class means are pushed far apart (large <em>S<sub>B</sub></em>).<br />\n(ii) Samples within each class are compact (small <em>S<sub>w</sub></em>).<br />\nHence, LDA explicitly seeks projection directions that maximize class separability, which directly corresponds to option (c).</p>"},{"n":65,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>A</em>, <em>B</em> and <em>C</em> be mutually independent events such that  <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/6e/6e3994c7a14896137797.png\" /> Let<em> q</em> be the probability that exactly two of these events occur. Find the value of 10 × <em>q</em> ________. (Upto 1 decimal place).</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3.8,3.8],"answerText":"3.8","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3.8</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2d/2d7e7f5283a4512cf541.png\" /></div>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":41,"NAT":14,"MSQ":10},"withoutKey":0}};
